WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.220
Hop on your bike for a freewheeling ride up the avenues of not-so-typical suburbia.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.980
This is Paperboy.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:30.580
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.380
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:33.800
And I'm Joe.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.980
Guys, this is one of those old-school jobs that doesn't really exist anymore, right?

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.380
Or am I out of touch?

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.740
I don't know. I mean, we gotta get the paper somehow.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:51.140
Both of my stepbrothers were once paperboys for the Buffalo News, but that's the last I have

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.120
had in any interaction with a paperboy.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.240
So, I don't know.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:01.640
It just seemed to be there was a time where every 12-year-old boy needed to become the paperboy.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:05.360
It was like, well, you're 12 now, so it's time to get on your bike and deliver paper.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.000
Was it 12? Holy shit.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.740
Well, because it's like a rite of passage, because then you have the training courses,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.060
and you have all the three fans in the crowd, and it's a big thing.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.540
Yeah. I remember when they would go through their training courses.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.400
Yeah. That's a coming-of-age story is really what the paperboy video game was.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.740
But, you know, we could talk about the game for a second, too, but I'm just thinking about

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.260
jobs that we had as kids.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:37.740
It is kind of funny how we make a lot more money now than we ever did at our childhood

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.540
jobs. But those jobs, it was like, man, when you got like $20 in your pocket, you could buy anything

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:44.600
you wanted. Oh, yeah.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.660
You felt rich.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.020
Especially, like, in high school.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.300
Like, about how many chicken tenders.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.320
You got cafeteria prices. Yeah.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.840
You go to Louie's, you get some chicken tenders with some ketchup.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.660
That's like three meals of Louie's right there.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.300
I'm thinking food, because I would just go right to the video games.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:09.240
Like, I remember saving up, like, okay, if I make, you know, even my lunch money.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.700
My parents were giving me $5 every day.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:12.820
I would just not eat.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.020
Yeah, I would just not eat.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.380
Instead, I would be like, I'm just going to save this $5.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.180
And in, you know.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.020
Was that the cost of your public school, like, lunch?

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.100
So, we had a situation at our high school where you could leave.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:31.020
You got to leave the high school and go out to eat for lunch.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.380
I forgot you told me about that. It was great.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:33.740
So, then I feel like you have the opposite problem.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.740
Where do you go for $5?

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.760
I think they were aware of that, though.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.720
I think most of the, you know, it's a true story, because there was a lot of people who had, like, you know, it was like a class system.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:47.860
You couldn't hang out with some kids because they had more money for lunch and you couldn't go to the places they went to.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.860
Oh, yeah. Your school was very different then because of that.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.340
Yeah. You couldn't hang with the rich kids that went to Applebee's every day.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:03:00.360
You couldn't. Instead, I actually, I lived close enough to the school, I just walked home and I would play, like, Guitar Hero

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.940
or Super Smash Brothers with some friends during the lunch break.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.300
Wow. It's a different world.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.900
I would just get $1.50 for lunch.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.220
That was great. And it was a good amount of food, too, for $1.50?

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.840
Wish I could go back.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.880
I don't even know how many 1904 Nathan's Hot Dogs that is.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:28.300
Anyway, Sean, you know a lot about Paperboy because both your brothers were Paperboys.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.680
So, you want to give us an overview on the game?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:40.000
All right. So, yeah, the game is, it's pretty much a simulation game of any paper delivery person's career.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.000
I know because sometimes I had to cover for my brothers when they couldn't do it.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:53.020
But basically, every day you would get, you'd get this printout of all of the places that are subscribed to the newspaper.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:04:00.440
But it didn't really matter because you knew based on the color of the house who was subscribed and who wasn't.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:17.500
So, you would, you know, take a nice bike ride down the street, the only street you were assigned, and try to avoid all of the crazy people and possessed objects on that street.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:27.840
And you would just try and throw your paper as hard as you could and hope you hit the door of the mailbox and you'd get points or money at the end of the day.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.680
Not when they mandated that anyone who didn't have a newspaper subscription had to paint their house red.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.100
Yeah, it happens.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.800
It happens. It makes you wonder though, who pays for that?

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.920
Is that the paper company that pays for that?

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.840
Or is that like a homeowners association?

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.340
It's definitely an HOA, yeah.

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.820
Okay, yeah. Gotcha, gotcha.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.340
You know what's interesting though?

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:57.820
You brought up a great point about how the game has, you know, even from the back of the box, not so typical suburbia of like, this idea that you're going to be a game.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:03.980
It's that, I don't know, most of the objects there are things that you might run into if you were biking down a street.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.540
And then all of a sudden there's also the Grim Reaper.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:12.200
Yeah. And a breakdancing dude in the middle of the sidewalk who shows up at the same place every single time.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:19.480
And then there's a dirt bike course at the end with an obstacle course, if you will, with like ramps and hoops to go through.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:31.820
You know, so it is like a fine blend between a simulation of a paper boy game and this like really fun, cute arcade game.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:42.600
Yeah, I mean, just in terms of the design of the obstacles being, you know, like the Grim Reaper and like maybe a crazy woman with a knife.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.800
Like, and I think in even like the arcade versions, was it a rolling pin?

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:56.940
There is someone with a rolling pin, but there's also someone definitely, I'm pretty sure a different person with a knife that's doing like a stabbing motion as he runs out.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.420
Yeah, when I was a kid, though, I was terrified of this game.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:06.460
It was a scary game to me because like, I think I remember the arcade version, like some of the houses looked haunted.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:17.140
And then like even in this version, there are there are like gravestones and you have those monsters like the Grim Reaper and a tornado.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.080
I kind of maybe thought that the guy that was breakdancing may have been a monster, too, when I was a kid.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:29.300
But like it was not fun to play this game for me when I was a child because I don't know.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.500
It just it just had a really weird, really weird tone going on.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:41.920
See, I had like a different, weirdly different experience that makes less sense because I play I used to play this game a lot and I forgot a lot about it.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:46.800
But when I when I was a kid, I don't remember like noticing that like these were weird obstacles.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:51.260
Like I don't remember I don't remember the Grim Reaper being there or like the tornado.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.080
I just totally blanked all that out.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.660
So I was playing it this time and it had been so long and it was kind of like that that like nostalgia.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.220
You forgot you had experience.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.360
Yeah, but then like all this other stuff came out and I was like, was this the same?

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.460
Did they make two NES versions?

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:06.840
But no, it was definitely this.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:08.000
I asked my brother about it, too.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.140
He remembers all the weird stuff.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:18.340
So I don't know why I blocked that stuff out, but I was actually kind of like it gave me a good laugh this time around because I was like, wow, this game is way more ridiculous than I remember it.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:27.500
But and it's not shy to just like have that be something that you become acquainted with right away upon starting the game.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:36.440
You know, you you you get to like maybe throw a newspaper to one normal house with maybe like a dog outside that you're like, oh, that dog's kind of scary.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.540
I'll stay away from being too close to the house.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.100
But, you know, for the most part, it's pretty normal.

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:44.520
And then like the next house, all of a sudden everything ramps up.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:48.040
It's like if you're in the street, boom, there's a car or the sewer grate.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:08:01.560
And then like, you know, like you said, the woman with the rolling pin comes out and charges at you or the tornado thing is very strange to me because it's also the world's smallest tornado localized entirely to the paper boy.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:04.400
And it's also the most unavoidable.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:06.280
Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:08.180
It just it just like chases you.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:09.200
It's targeted at you.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:13.280
It does not care if the lawnmower or the RC toy is nearby.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.880
Like, I mean, you know, we're constantly listing off objects here.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.440
And I think it is pretty cool that they managed to include like so many different things.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:29.180
And it really makes this a very creative world that takes all of two minutes to like understand.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:39.860
Yeah. Yeah. And I'll say even there were a lot of there were a few obstacles like the rolling tire that just turns randomly or the tornado that seems at first like it's impossible to avoid.

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:45.220
That at first I was thinking like, well, this is kind of this is like a little bit of a damper in this game.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.240
This is like a point against it because that some of the obstacles are like unfair.

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.500
But then for a lot of them, I did sort of learn.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.840
Like, I think that's part of it.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:02.600
Like at first, for some reason, because some of them are like recognizable objects, I don't give them like that video game benefit of the doubt that like I have to learn how to beat them.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.280
So so at first I was frustrated.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.560
But then once I've kind of figured out, oh, like I kind of see the pattern in the way the tire moves.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:13.960
And I and I found a little strategy to get away from the tornado.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:19.140
And once you like learn that stuff, it actually feels kind of fair for most of them.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:26.880
Well, there's no reason to have to like learn any, you know, like like the whole game is just you in constant motion.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.060
It's an auto scroller, right?

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.400
Where you're just constantly going forward.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:36.240
And it is like the anti shmup, you know, like, yeah, you can throw newspapers at some of these things to like stop them in their tracks.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:42.000
But for the most part, you just have to constantly dodge everything and hope to deliver the papers, too.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:47.760
Because don't forget, that's the actual point of the game is throwing the newspapers onto the stoops.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:59.140
But that is a good point, though, because like you're trying to conserve your ammunition, which are the the meet the newspapers, because you never really know when you're going to get a new bundle.

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:15.800
And if you're trying to get like extra points by not just delivering papers to the people you deliver them to, but knock over trash cans and gravestones and break the break the non subscribers windows, which is encouraged.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.460
You could end up with no new no newspapers and skipping houses you need to hit.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:29.300
So, yeah, it's it's like you're just trying to conserve resources during this very hectic jump down the street.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:34.900
And then there's the whole, you know, timing aspect of when to throw the papers.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:41.360
I think, you know, for the objects that are obstacles that are in your way, that's a little easier because you're very close to those things.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:52.500
But if you're hanging out at the sidewalk or in the street, there's like this weird delay of when you think you should be throwing the paper versus when you should actually be throwing the paper.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:11:03.380
If you want it to land on the stoop or for even more points in the mailbox, which is a really cool feature that like it won't count if you just throw it on the lawn and get it like in the vicinity.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:07.560
And it definitely won't count if you break a subscribers window like that's going to hurt you.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:19.860
But it's neat that they have like two different variables for like scoring points in the sense of like you don't always have to just get it in the mailbox because I feel like that would be a very tough objective.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:23.880
But the stoop is not necessarily this gigantic goalpost either.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:25.300
Like it still is a tight spot.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:32.440
I was going to say, I do think that like the getting the feel of that is very satisfying as well.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:36.420
And there's also like interesting little ways to cheese it.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:39.240
I actually tested this pretty extensively.

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:49.940
And I'm and I'm pretty certain at this point that once you've thrown the paper, it scrolls forward at the speed you're scrolling forward.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:55.940
So if the paper is in the air going horizontally towards the house and you slow down, the paper will slow down its vertical motion.

00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:58.360
So you can sort of like cheese it a little.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:05.240
If it's like going close to the mailbox, you can slow down to like pull it back a little bit or speed up to push it forward a little bit.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:10.520
But I did a few times where I like went through and like as soon as I get to this line, I'm going to throw the paper.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:11.680
This time I'm going to go really slow.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.600
Next time I do the same thing, I go really fast and there's a noticeable difference.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:16.780
Well, I never tried that.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.260
I was very bad at this game.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:24.860
This is a this is a pretty tough game, even though it presents itself as kind of casual.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:30.420
But I broke more subscribers windows that I that I ever needed to.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.720
And even I was aiming for my non subscribers windows.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.120
I was just hitting the wall.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:43.040
That whole situation with the newspaper traveling at the speed that you're going, I never really put that together.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.900
I was just so curious about it the whole time.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:51.880
I was trying to figure it out, but it's so hard to tell until you just like do the do the same thing over and over again and see what the difference is.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:54.040
Yeah, but it was significant.

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:57.560
So I think it actually would be like a.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.660
A strategy for the speed runners out there.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:06.680
Well, I also think, Joe, that you is just a strategy like speed running or not.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:14.240
Like, I agree that it makes sense, even though, you know, you wouldn't be able to just because you're riding your bike fast, you wouldn't be able to throw faster.

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:23.380
I think it makes sense in the gameplay point of view that it would be weird if no matter what speed you were going, the newspapers always traveled at the same length.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.020
Like there's something satisfying about the momentum of the character carrying the newspaper with it.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.100
But it's not it.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:34.100
But it's actually it's not the realistic way that you're talking about the way you're talking about would make more realistic sense.

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:39.700
But I'm saying that once the paper is already in the air, you can still slow it down or speed it up.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:41.600
Oh, I didn't catch that the first time.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:45.880
Yeah, that sounds like that sounds like it might even just be limited to the NES version of the game.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:49.460
I don't remember that happening in other versions of this game.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.780
Yeah. Also, speed runners probably the wrong maybe like like metagamer.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:57.160
Not necessarily speed runner, but metagamer.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:58.740
Going for the high score. Yeah.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.900
So, Sean, you said you had a tough time with the game.

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:08.880
Did it have any did it have anything to do with the massive hitbox that you are as a character?

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.640
100 percent. Yeah.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:18.980
So when you're there are two like lanes, there's the sidewalk and, you know, the lawn.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.200
If you can navigate all the lawn ornaments and stuff.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:29.000
And then there's the street and you can only cross over between the lawn and the street or at least.

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:31.300
From the street to the to the sidewalk.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:38.360
When there is like a driveway or wherever a curb is not.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:44.080
And it always seems to be that if you get stuck on the street, you're going to run into a car.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:53.420
And there isn't really enough room on the street to avoid cars or or like the God forbid you get a car coming at you.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:56.440
While there's also a sewer grate coming up.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:04.920
And it just seems to be like it's just inevitable that you're going to get hit and you're going to fall over and you lose one of your precious three lives.

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.880
Um, that wasn't cool.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:16.880
And even when you're on the sidewalk and you have to navigate around like, uh, plants and dogs and skateboarders.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:24.860
Because it just seems like the, uh, yeah, the hit boxes don't really conform to the sprites very well.

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:35.440
Yeah, I feel like the to the your point about like the street being small because the street just takes up like to just to paint a picture for anyone who hasn't played it.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:40.880
The game is kind of scrolling diagonally at the bottom right corner, just the smallest part of the bottom right corner.

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:53.160
And even like the whole area that you can because of the way you're throwing the papers across the screen to the houses, the entire area that you can occupy is like it almost it almost feels like less than half the screen.

00:15:53.980 --> 00:15:55.320
Oh, it's definitely less.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:57.960
So it's very tight. Yeah.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:04.740
And I would I would argue there, though, that there is there is probably some conscious decision on that, too.

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:08.460
You know, assuming that it's an arcade game that's supposed to eat your quarters.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:18.760
Sean, you mentioned that there's like the lawns, too, that you are able to bike on, but they're filled with various lawn objects or like obstacles in your way.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:25.160
So that's not really that encouraged, but it is a path to go to try to get around sidewalk obstacles.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:28.480
But then there's the sidewalk where you're supposed to be, in my opinion.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:37.500
I feel like, you know, for the best way to, you know, hit the stoops and to avoid most things like you want to be on the sidewalk for the most part.

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:46.640
But if you have to go into the street to Joe's point, yeah, it's only like it's only like 20 percent of the entire screen gives you the street.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:49.240
But that's like a do or die moment for you.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:54.360
It's like, you know, never ride your bike in the street, kids, because now anything can happen.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.620
Like now that you're in that street, very little control over what's coming at you.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:16:59.080
You won't be able to see it in time.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:03.580
So only go into the street if you think you have a quick entrance back onto the sidewalk.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:05.340
I feel like there is a lesson there.

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:09.460
Because it's also apparently like the street is only the oncoming lane.

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:16.940
There's no, you're never traveling with traffic or there are cars that are parked in the oncoming lane.

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:28.440
But yeah, no, I mean, it definitely is like a tiered thing where you're only supposed to be down there if you have somehow run out of options on the sidewalk.

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.800
Yeah, I did feel like maybe it wasn't quite so intentional, though.

00:17:34.180 --> 00:17:39.880
Because I did feel like there were a lot of times where it felt like, OK, these enemies, it's better if I'm in the street and these enemies better from the sidewalk.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:46.920
