Yeah, I think you play through Sunday, actually.

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:33.740
I thought you weren't going to, but I'm pretty sure you do.

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:38.100
I can't remember. You just start something on day again?

00:33:38.100 --> 00:33:39.100
I think Sunday's just another day.

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:40.920
No, I think Sunday's another day, and that's the end.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:42.480
It definitely ends after the week.

00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:44.120
I'm pretty sure it ends on Sunday and not Saturday.

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:45.620
Unless I just didn't do good enough,

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:47.960
maybe it would keep going if you had, like, a higher score or something.

00:33:49.440 --> 00:33:51.740
What does the newspaper say on that Sunday, though, Joe?

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.640
It says, well, so if you finish it

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:56.540
and you don't, like, lose or lose all your subscribers,

00:33:56.920 --> 00:33:58.260
it says something like,

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:01.620
like, Paperboy retires in glory or something.

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:03.640
Something that I did want to get to,

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:04.700
like, this game is funny.

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:06.780
Like, it's intentionally funny.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:08.740
Like, in a lot, like, the newspapers,

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.420
the little articles, like, saying, like,

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:13.780
Paperboy retires in glory,

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:14.880
and then underneath it says, like,

00:34:15.560 --> 00:34:16.900
Mysterious Vandal never caught

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:18.080
because you broke all those windows

00:34:18.080 --> 00:34:19.160
and, like, things like that.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:20.700
But even, like, the...

00:34:21.200 --> 00:34:22.640
I mean, obviously, there's the obvious stuff

00:34:22.640 --> 00:34:24.060
where, like, suddenly, like, on day one,

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:25.200
there's, like, a dog chasing you,

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:27.180
and then on day two, the Grim Reaper's chasing you.

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:30.620
Like, that's funny. But also, like, you get to the end of the...

00:34:30.620 --> 00:34:32.160
The obstacle course at all,

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:33.880
I know it's part of the game

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:35.220
and it adds to the gameplay,

00:34:35.320 --> 00:34:37.680
but I think... I really feel like they add that

00:34:38.080 --> 00:34:39.540
also because they thought it was funny.

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:41.300
Especially because you get to the end

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.520
and there's two full sets of bleachers

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:46.140
with only three people in it

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:48.140
just going wild for the Paperboy.

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:49.900
And then the Paperboy just, like,

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:51.500
does, like, a bow. Like, it's a...

00:34:51.500 --> 00:34:53.240
And it's just really funny to me.

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:55.700
Like, oh, these three major Paperboy fans

00:34:55.700 --> 00:34:57.920
are just going nuts in these empty bleachers.

00:34:58.340 --> 00:35:00.140
It felt very intentionally funny to me.

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:03.660
Joe, you know, I am glad you brought up

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:05.620
the comic aspect of the game, too,

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:08.400
because that is something that we missed.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:12.780
You know, forget about, like, the newspaper is something that is very funny.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:14.640
You know, it starts the game,

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:15.900
but it also ends the game.

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:17.380
That is something that, like,

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:18.760
they've continued throughout

00:35:18.760 --> 00:35:20.540
the multiple ports of this game

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:22.840
to, like, change the copy

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:23.960
of what that says.

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:25.460
And some versions get, like,

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:28.440
really extreme with the game over sequence.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:29.780
Some will just say, like,

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:33.200
you're fired. Others will say Paperboy calls it quits.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:34.500
This version, I think.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:37.580
Yeah. There's one get well soon, Paperboy.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:39.700
One crash too many, says Doctor.

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:42.540
Headline news. It's front page news

00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:43.500
is whatever's going on

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:44.640
with the local Paperboy.

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:47.120
There's one for the Apple II

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:48.980
that is just extra, extra,

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:50.860
you are fired.

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:52.140
And that's it.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:54.320
Nothing else.

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:55.360
Everything else is like, blah, blah, blah.

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:58.140
That's how they tell you. They take out an ad in the newspaper

00:35:58.140 --> 00:35:59.140
to tell you you're fired.

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:01.440
So, you know,

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:02.880
there's that element of it, too,

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:04.280
but the art style, too, is cute.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.160
You know? Everything is, as I mentioned,

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:08.500
like, kind of to scale.

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:12.100
There's no, like, ridiculously oversized object.

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.600
Except for the tornado. The tornado is definitely, like,

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:15.940
what is that? Is that a tornado?

00:36:16.280 --> 00:36:17.420
It's like a little dirt devil.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.140
Dirt devil. No, it's even smaller than that.

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:23.200
I don't know. Yeah, and then there's, like,

00:36:23.360 --> 00:36:24.200
you know, the cat

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:25.980
and stuff like that. The sewer grates.

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:27.880
I think in some versions of the game,

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.440
I don't recall seeing the NES one,

00:36:29.600 --> 00:36:30.680
but in some versions,

00:36:31.500 --> 00:36:32.780
there was a manhole,

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:33.920
and a guy will, like,

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:35.320
peek out with eyes.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:36.780
Oh, that's not the NES one?

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:38.700
Okay. Yeah, I died on that a lot.

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:40.080
Yeah, I love that, though. Because it's on the street.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:42.920
That's funny. Yeah, yeah.

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.640
What's he doing? What's he doing down there?

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:46.700
Yes, there is something,

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:47.860
also just, like,

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:49.600
there's a certain sense of joy

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.420
from just, you know,

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:52.660
being the paperboy.

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:54.740
I'm still scared. I'm still scared of this neighborhood.

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:56.080
That's true, too, though.

00:36:56.120 --> 00:36:58.320
You can be scared. I can understand how some kids

00:36:58.320 --> 00:36:59.860
would grow up scared

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:00.740
of being a paperboy

00:37:00.740 --> 00:37:31.360
because of this. I can understand how some kids

00:37:38.380 --> 00:37:40.200
So, let me just tell you guys

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:41.740
about the arcade version

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:44.420
of this game. If for no other reason

00:37:44.420 --> 00:37:46.140
than that,

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:47.740
instead of having, like,

00:37:47.800 --> 00:37:49.100
a joystick or,

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:50.340
you know,

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:51.440
the normal buttons

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:52.960
that you would have at an arcade machine

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:55.580
at the time, in this arcade machine,

00:37:55.580 --> 00:37:57.520
the joystick are actual

00:37:57.520 --> 00:37:59.000
bike handlebars.

00:38:05.640 --> 00:38:06.760
and then the

00:38:06.760 --> 00:38:17.500
and then the

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:20.720
and then the

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:31.080
tabletop of the arcade itself,

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:32.760
that'd be bad. So instead,

00:38:33.320 --> 00:38:35.220
there are buttons on each side

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:37.100
of the handlebars that you use to throw

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:41.180
the papers. It's still just one side

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:44.640
of the street. But

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:47.080
I think that's cool that you

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:49.180
accelerate by pushing the

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:51.320
bars forward and you pull back

00:38:51.320 --> 00:38:52.700
with the brakes. So

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:55.620
in a way, this handlebar

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:57.020
is the entire control

00:38:57.020 --> 00:38:58.320
of the game.

00:38:58.520 --> 00:39:01.500
You're just really a motion-based arcade

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:02.900
game in the early 80s.

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:05.340
Yeah, I'd prefer to play that version.

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:07.400
Yeah, I watched some

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.260
videos of it. I actually didn't realize that

00:39:09.260 --> 00:39:10.360
that's how it was being controlled.

00:39:11.240 --> 00:39:13.440
It does look like it controls a lot

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:17.300
smoother. And deep buried inside

00:39:17.300 --> 00:39:19.400
the arcade game, if you look into

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:21.480
the code, there is a message

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:23.380
from one of the

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:25.480
programmers of the game that says

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.820
steal this code and you die.

00:39:28.100 --> 00:39:29.300
Atari 1985

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:32.580
JFS. Very threatening.

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:35.520
And, you know, that was a thing, though,

00:39:35.720 --> 00:39:37.780
that was happening specifically to

00:39:38.780 --> 00:39:39.820
Atari and

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:42.440
Namco. Their arcade games were being

00:39:42.440 --> 00:39:43.320
essentially, like,

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:45.940
stolen and then put it under

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:47.880
new names that were very similar

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:48.700
in-game. game.

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:50.100
And I think

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:51.700
Atari was the first one to actually

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:53.040
win, like, a lawsuit

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:55.880
being like, yeah, you can say that

00:39:55.880 --> 00:39:56.840
that's a different, like,

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:57.940
you can title it differently

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:59.460
and you can color it differently.

00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:00.340
That's our code.

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.980
And so, you can't make that game anymore.

00:40:03.100 --> 00:40:04.740
And they, like, won the rights to that.

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:06.920
I don't know. It seems kind of hypocritical

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:08.960
of Atari because they've done

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:09.940
their own shady stuff.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:10.940
I mean, isn't Tengen

00:40:10.940 --> 00:40:12.220
just a way to get around,

00:40:12.240 --> 00:40:13.500
like, Nintendo things?

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:16.200
100%. So, you know,

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:17.280
check yourself, Atari.

00:40:18.460 --> 00:40:21.820
Yeah. And if anybody is interested

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:23.400
in the,

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:25.700
like, like a really good deep dive

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:26.560
onto Paperboy,

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:27.720
I'm not going to cover it here,

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:30.220
but the Game Developers Conference,

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:32.420
like, I think now

00:40:32.420 --> 00:40:33.540
three or four years ago,

00:40:33.800 --> 00:40:34.980
had the co-creator

00:40:34.980 --> 00:40:35.920
of Paperboy

00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:37.340
come on and do, like,

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.040
an hour-long lecture

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:40.940
on how they, like,

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:42.100
program the game

00:40:42.100 --> 00:40:43.040
and design the game

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:44.180
and their philosophy

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:45.580
behind the way

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:47.060
they do things. And I will say,

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:48.500
like, I didn't watch all of it,

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:49.980
but that's the one

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:51.000
that made me think,

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:52.640
you know, who am I

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:54.080
to be picking on some of these things

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:55.760
that I want to change

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:57.240
the game of because they were clearly,

00:40:57.460 --> 00:40:58.840
like, thought of

00:40:58.840 --> 00:41:00.420
as elements of the game

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:01.540
that they purposefully

00:41:01.540 --> 00:41:02.560
wanted there. It's not,

00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:04.340
it's not programming errors

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:05.700
or just difficulty induced.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.040
Like, they wanted the obstacles

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:09.060
to be threatening

00:41:09.060 --> 00:41:10.660
to the Paperboy.

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:15.140
Makes sense. Believe it or not,

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:16.920
there are sequels to Paperboy.

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:19.420
Why not Paperboy 2?

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:21.300
We're going to play that,

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:22.660
right? We're going to play that,

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:24.300
Sean, on the NES itself.

00:41:24.600 --> 00:41:26.080
I don't know what you'd do differently.

00:41:26.860 --> 00:41:28.360
Maybe you just amp it up,

00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:29.400
like, 100%.

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:30.600
You just say, you know what?

00:41:30.800 --> 00:41:31.740
Paperboy was crazy

00:41:31.740 --> 00:41:33.200
and it had an obstacle course.

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:35.220
So, in Paperboy 2,

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.020
you're going to be in a monster truck rally,

00:41:38.060 --> 00:41:38.960
but as,

00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:40.960
on a bike. Is that a thing?

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:42.520
No, absolutely not.

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:43.740
Okay. I don't know what they're going to do.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:44.720
I didn't want to spoil it

00:41:44.720 --> 00:41:45.700
because I imagine,

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:47.360
I imagine they're not going to change

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:48.980
throwing papers to houses,

00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:51.680
right? Like, that's the whole conceit

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:52.840
of the game. So, I think the only thing

00:41:52.840 --> 00:41:55.060
they can really do is just make it more ridiculous

00:41:55.060 --> 00:41:56.640
in terms of obstacles

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:57.960
and design.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:41:59.840
Well, I was thinking maybe

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:00.700
there would be, like,

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:03.140
a secondary, a secondary mode

00:42:03.140 --> 00:42:04.020
where you have to go

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.060
and collect the bill

00:42:05.060 --> 00:42:05.920
after,

00:42:07.320 --> 00:42:08.660
like, after each week

00:42:08.660 --> 00:42:09.500
and then you'd have to do

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:10.980
some, like, business management

00:42:11.800 --> 00:42:13.280
to talk prices.

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:14.920
You know what? I'm going to make this.

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:16.140
Yeah, it's like a career mode.

00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:18.480
Yeah. Yeah,

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:20.040
or maybe it's an open world

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:21.460
game. Open world game.

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:24.560
Yeah, Yeah. Where you can freely explore

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:26.660
the entire continental United States

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:29.720
on your bike, but you have to,

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:31.660
you have to advertise yourself first

00:42:31.660 --> 00:42:32.720
as the Paperboy.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:33.940
Yeah, it's a freelance Paperboy.

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:37.020
Yep. And that's that.

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:39.500
And then we have Paperboy 64,

00:42:40.380 --> 00:42:41.220
which came out,

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:42.980
believe it or not, on the Nintendo 64.

00:42:44.060 --> 00:42:46.200
And I didn't even know

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:48.520
about this one. I can't quite tell

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:49.380
if it's a remake

00:42:50.140 --> 00:42:51.100
of Paperboy,

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:52.080
but just in 3D.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:53.200
But thankfully,

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:54.940
if everybody remembers

00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:56.160
a friend of the show

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:57.820
and occasional voice

00:42:57.820 --> 00:42:59.460
of the show, Sam Smith,

00:42:59.460 --> 00:43:00.780
who also designs

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:02.180
the theme song

00:43:02.180 --> 00:43:03.040
for Nesta. That,

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:06.260
you have him to thank. He wrote a little blurb

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:07.560
to me here. He could be on the show today,

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:09.580
but he said, when I was little,

00:43:09.820 --> 00:43:10.860
my parents gave me

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:11.740
Paperboy 64

00:43:11.740 --> 00:43:12.880
for Hanukkah.

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:14.180
I was super excited

00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:15.060
to play a new game,

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:16.880
so I gave it a shot. Immediately,

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:18.700
I knew something was wrong.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:20.740
The graphics go beyond

00:43:20.740 --> 00:43:22.380
crappy early 3D graphics

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:24.460
and into barely polygonal

00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:26.800
smiling stick figure horror.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:28.460
I can't stress how

00:43:28.460 --> 00:43:29.540
uncomfortable this game

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:31.600
is to look at. Like if Psychonauts

00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:33.020
tried to take itself seriously,

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:34.340
the first level

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:35.400
is a training level

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:37.520
where you learn the clunky 3D controls

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:38.480
throwing papers

00:43:38.480 --> 00:43:39.560
and doing sweet tricks

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:41.460
on your bike like a rad 90s kid.

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:43.360
All the levels are on rails,

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:45.080
culminating in a boss fight

00:43:45.080 --> 00:43:46.220
where you have to take down,

00:43:46.480 --> 00:43:48.580
I think, Frankenstein's monster?

00:43:48.920 --> 00:43:50.340
The only saving grace

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:51.620
for the game was that it had

00:43:51.620 --> 00:43:52.920
a memory pack manager

00:43:52.920 --> 00:43:54.560
built in and it was the only way

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:55.960
I could edit my save data

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:57.080
for all my games.

